AUDIO: 'This is Open Wiki, a brand new tool from the team at Langchain, and it\'s quietly solving one of the most annoying problems in AI coding. It\'s a command line tool that automatically writes and maintains a full wiki of documentation for your codebase. Built specifically so'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your AI coding agent actually understands your project. And here\'s the kicker. It launched barely two weeks ago and already has over 7,000 stars. For a category this new, that kind of traction'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'almost never happens by accident. It\'s open source under the MIT license written in Typescript and backed by Langchain, which means it isn\'t some weekend project that vanishes next month. The idea is simple but sharp.'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Your AI agent is only as good as the context you give it. And most code bases have terrible outofdate docs or no docs at all. Open Wiki fixes exactly that. Lang Chain has spent years watching'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'developers wire language models into real software so they know precisely where AI coding falls apart and stale context is right at the top of that list. Having that team behind a tool like this is a real signal that the'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'problem it targets is a serious lasting one. And stick around because there\'s one genuinely clever move it makes that gets your agent to actually read these docs instead of ignoring them completely. Think about how your coding agent works today. You ask it to change'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something and it burns time and tokens gripping around your repo, opening files half at random, trying to reverse engineer how your project actually fits together. Half the time it guesses wrong, breaks a convention or reinvents'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a helper you already have. Now imagine that instead it opens a clean, accurate wiki that explains your architecture, your modules, and your patterns up front. That is the whole difference between an agent that fumbles and one'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that actually knows your codebase. It\'s a weirdly invisible tax. Every single session, your agent relearned your project from scratch, and every wrong guess cost you a correction, a reprompt, and a little more of your patience.'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Multiply that across a whole team, and it becomes a genuinely expensive habit hiding in plain sight. And developers notice fast. Over 7,000 to 400 GitHub stars in roughly 2 weeks is an absolutely explosive start. The kind of'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'curve you only ever see when a tool hits a real nerve. Just about everyone building with AI agents has felt this exact pain. and Open Wiki showing up with a clean answer to it is why the project took off almost overnight.'

[02:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Context tooling for agents is one of the hottest corners of developer software right now. And Open Wiki launching straight to the top of it says the timing was just about perfect. So how does it actually work? You point Open'

[02:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Wiki at a repository and it reads through your whole codebase and writes structured documentation into a dedicated Open Wiki folder. Think architecture overviews, module summaries, and how all the pieces connect all in clean markdown that lives'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right alongside your code. When your code changes, it refreshes that documentation instead of letting it quietly rot. It is genuinely the docs you always meant to write but never actually had the time for. And because'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it all lives in plain markdown inside your repo, you can read it, edit it, and review it in a pull request like any other file. Nothing is hidden away in a database or a separate service. The documentation becomes a first-class part'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of your codebase that both humans and agents can lean on. Getting started is refreshingly simple. You install it globally from npm. Run open wiki init to pick your model and drop in an API key and it generates your first wiki. From'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then on, open wiki update keeps everything current. And here\'s that clever move I promised. Open wiki automatically appends instructions to your agents MD and claude.m MD files the exact files your coding agent already'

[03:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reads telling it to consult the wiki whenever it needs context so your agent starts using the docs without you lifting a single finger. You can also just talk to it directly asking it in plain English to document a specific'

[03:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'part of the repo or fire a oneshot command and have it print an answer and exit. It\'s built to fit however you already work. Beyond the basics, this is where Open Wiki gets serious. It drops in a ready-made CI workflow for GitHub'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actions or GitLab. So, every time your code changes, it opens a pull request that updates the docs automatically. Your wiki literally maintains itself. It\'s model flexible, too, working with Open Router, Open AI, Anthropic,'

[04:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Fireworks, and any Open AI compatible endpoint, so you\'re never locked to one single vendor. And it can optionally trace every run to Langsmith if you want to see exactly what it did and why. That'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
